Differences Between Multithreading and Multiprocessing:


🔄 Multithreading
Definition: Multiple threads run within a single process.

Memory: Threads share the same memory space.

Communication: Easier and faster (since memory is shared).

Performance: Better for I/O-bound or lightweight tasks.

Overhead: Less memory usage; lower overhead.

Crash Impact: One thread crash may affect other threads (since same process).

Java Support: Native support using Thread, Runnable, ExecutorService.



🧠 Multiprocessing
Definition: Multiple independent processes run simultaneously.

Memory: Each process has its own memory space.

Communication: More complex (uses Inter-Process Communication like sockets or files).

Performance: Better for CPU-bound tasks; avoids GIL in some languages like Python.

Overhead: More memory usage and overhead.

Crash Impact: One process crash doesn’t affect others.

Java Support: Achievable using multiple JVMs or ProcessBuilder.


🔁 Summary Table:
| Feature         | Multithreading               | Multiprocessing        |
| --------------- | ---------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| Process Count   | Single process               | Multiple processes     |
| Memory Usage    | Shared memory                | Separate memory        |
| Communication   | Easy and fast                | Slower and complex     |
| Overhead        | Low                          | High                   |
| Use Case        | Lightweight, concurrent apps | Heavy computation apps |
| Fault Isolation | Poor                         | Strong                 |


